Internal generation, variable properties and hot spots (@th-generation: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer, #source "../sources/A-Heat-Transfer-Textbook-Lienhard.lingua#r_7TKQ2D5YRNQ8XVHXNJQDRANWKD", #source "../sources/Second-Level-Heat-Transfer-Vedula.lingua#r_663BY2CF56BBB35C1S595A2VRF", #source "../sources/FiPy-Conservative-Finite-Volume-Methods.lingua#r_5C155DKGJ5WYGV18EJ1SC41G0Q", #thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer "Thermal-contacts-layers-and-material-history.lingua#r_68Z22QE65T6P6TYMDN3CP6500E") { r_2SW0JFBWDHREWM93VCFK7GY5M0
**Read:** [A Heat Transfer Textbook — Lienhard and Lienhard](../sources/A-Heat-Transfer-Textbook-Lienhard.lingua#r_7TKQ2D5YRNQ8XVHXNJQDRANWKD), generation and variable-property conduction; [Second Level Course in Heat Transfer — R. P. Vedula](../sources/Second-Level-Heat-Transfer-Vedula.lingua#r_663BY2CF56BBB35C1S595A2VRF), steady generation and nonlinear considerations; [FiPy — conservative finite-volume modelling](../sources/FiPy-Conservative-Finite-Volume-Methods.lingua#r_5C155DKGJ5WYGV18EJ1SC41G0Q), source terms and nonlinear iteration.

With internal energy generation, heat rate changes across the material and the maximum temperature can occur away from a boundary. Derive the temperature profile for a uniformly generating slab or cylinder, apply the actual boundary conditions and verify that total outward heat equals integrated generation at steady state.

Electrical, chemical, nuclear or dissipative sources may depend on temperature or other fields. When the source grows faster than heat can be removed, a steady solution can disappear or become unstable. A converged low-temperature branch is not automatically the realized operating state.

For variable k(T), preserve ∇·(k∇T) rather than treating k∇²T as generally identical. Compare an analytically integrable case with a conservative numerical result. Identify maximum material/contact temperature, not merely average outlet temperature, when assessing a thermal service.

